### Format: Read the inputs from stdin solve the problem and write the answer to stdout (do not directly test on the sample inputs). Enclose your code within delimiters as follows. Ensure that when the python program runs, it reads the inputs, runs the algorithm and writes output to STDOUT.



PLAN:
Model a placement by binary variables on the midpoints of cell edges, where 1 means the tile has an endpoint there; the no-dead-end condition makes the two variables on each shared edge equal. For each cell, type A forces exactly one vertical and one horizontal endpoint, while type B forces either both vertical or both horizontal endpoints. This gives a vertical propagation rule: moving down a column, the vertical edge bit flips on A and stays on B, so every column must contain an even number of A's. For a fixed row, the horizontal edge bits form a cycle with xor difference 1 at A cells and 0 at B cells; hence each row must also have an even number of A's. If a row is all A it has two independent horizontal choices; otherwise its B cells fix the horizontal cycle and impose xor constraints among the initial vertical bits of the columns at those B positions. Maintain column parities while scanning rows, add the row constraints to a parity DSU over the W initial vertical bits, and if all parities and DSU are consistent the answer is 2^{components + all-A rows} modulo 998244353.

NOTES:

## ideation
The central difficulty is that local tile rotations are not independent: an endpoint on a shared edge must be mirrored by the neighboring tile, and the torus makes every row and column wrap around into a cycle. If endpoint presence is encoded as bits on the midpoints of grid edges, the no-dead-end rule identifies the two bits on each shared edge. With that encoding, an A tile says the two vertical edge bits differ and the two horizontal edge bits differ; a B tile says the two vertical bits are equal, the two horizontal bits are equal, and the vertical bit is the complement of the horizontal bit. All of this is linear over GF(2). The torus wrap-around immediately gives parity conditions: the number of A tiles in every row and every column must be even. After propagating vertical bits down columns and horizontal bits across rows, the only remaining coupling is at B cells, where a row variable and a column variable are related by a fixed xor label. Thus the counting problem becomes a labeled graph 2-coloring / component-counting problem, and the answer is either 0 or a power of two. The main risks are convention errors in prefix parities, missing the torus parity checks, mishandling all-A rows/columns, parity-DSU merge errors, and performance/input overhead for many small test cases.


